```cpp
class Point {
public:
    int x, y;
    Point(int initialX, int initialY) : x(initialX), y(initialY) {}
};
```
A parameterized constructor takes one or more arguments. Member initializer lists (e.g., `: x(initialX), y(initialY)`) are preferred for initialization over assignment inside the constructor body, especially for `const` members, references, or members of classes with no default constructor.

A delegating constructor allows one constructor to call another constructor of the *same class* to perform initializations. This helps avoid code duplication and improves maintainability by centralizing common initialization logic.
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    int value;
    MyClass(int v) : value(v) {} // Target constructor
    MyClass() : MyClass(0) {}    // Delegating constructor
};
```
Here, `MyClass()` delegates to `MyClass(int)` with `0` as the argument.
